 The important rule remains stricter than fallback-based optimisation: AGC may use more cores for read-only preparation and duplicate-work elimination, but Bukkit-visible mutation, plugin callbacks, redstone, entity ticking and chunk commit ordering stay in ordered lanes.
+
+
+## AGC 26.2 alpha14 - Wild-scale kernel remodel
+
+Alpha14 makes the large-survival target the primary optimisation shape: thousands of ordinary survival players at 20 TPS while preserving vanilla/Paper gameplay feel and plugin semantics. The new `AGCScaleKernel` reserves CPU for the primary thread, Netty, GC, OS and plugins before distributing remaining capacity to invisible work only.
+
+Major alpha14 components:
+
+- `AGCScaleKernel`: central budget kernel for thousand-player survival scale.
+- `AGCRegionHotspotMap`: tick-local spawn/town hotspot accounting for shared read-only planning.
+- `AGCNetworkFanoutKernel`: packet-shape fanout planning without packet drops or connection reorder.
+- `AGCChunkPipelineKernel`: chunk intent token planning while preserving FIFO and Paper chunk completion order.
+- `AGCEntityTrackerKernel`: read-only entity candidate preparation without skipping entity tick, AI, damage or interactions.
+- `AGCThreadAffinityTranslator` and `AGCPluginLogicTranslator`: explicit translator layers that convert plugin/game logic into read-only prepare or ordered primary-thread commit phases.
+
+The hard rules remain unchanged: no packet drops, no chunk FIFO skips, no entity tick skips, no off-thread Bukkit mutation, no plugin event reorder, no redstone/farm/block-entity semantic changes. `/agc wild-scale` reports the new kernel.
